#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125368} :activity/id #uuid "ca8227e6-ef47-5702-8c4f-321ab31e8355" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729 :topic/id #uuid "b90e497c-df74-56b9-a5d7-98c7bdb922cb" :topic/math-academy-id 1920} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125899 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a straight line passing through the point $P(3,-1,7)$ and that's parallel to the direction vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ As we know, we can write the vector equation of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nUsing the vector equation above, we can create a set of **parametric equations** where we express a general point $(x,y,z)$ in terms of  the parameter $t.$\n\nIf we let $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ be the position vector of the general point $(x,y,z),$ then the above equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1+5t \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=-1+5t \\\\ z=7-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equations in the above system are called the **parametric equations** of the straight line.\nGeometrically, the equation states that a point belongs to the line if and only if its coordinates have the form\n\n$$\n(3, \\: -1+5t, \\: 7-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n![](images/e2/e29ce95cdccb47b97578f6431a96e608f7af9f6709410b221ff517b436896608.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ff73cc8-91a0-5a25-9c14-6e87496adac8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "de4015ef-1002-54f7-adee-e5eee4e87979" :step/math-academy-id 189149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124498 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124227 :question/id #uuid "001af492-25b1-5f75-b030-668708eee118" :question/math-academy-id "e-15602"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400296}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"} :step/id #uuid "1c0d5ba6-1e60-5d4d-b42e-60ba32609db6" :step/math-academy-id 189150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124499 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124228 :question/id #uuid "7a6038c2-3f7b-5dfa-a9e2-256b246fcc20" :question/math-academy-id "e-15603"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3699391-68e8-5c59-8ca2-83fded9f22de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398298}] :kno